well brian people have questions after  the nevada department of wildlife  decision to euthanize a mountain lion in  the southwest valley  now the department is now explaining why  it came to that decision on tuesday  joe muller is live at the apartment  complex where it happened at flamingo  and hualapai joe  well here at this location we're about a  mile and a half away from the edge of  town and it was early yesterday when  that mountain line made its way  to this apartment complex you see behind  us here after reportedly trying to say  they  sedated multiple times wildlife  officials say they needed to do  something before someone here got hurt  i saw the back of it going under the car  i said damn i look big  this urban apartment complex is the last  place you would expect to see a mountain  lion  we saw a guy walking his dog and we was  like sir was like hold on  you know it's a mountain lion out here  kendrick jones says he was shocked by  the sighting  i don't know how he got in there but it  was terrifying for a minute  jones says it took two hours to get the  mountain lion out nevada department of  wildlife officials say it was euthanized  at the apartment complex  that decision sparked outrage on social  media many people questioning why the  cat wasn't sedated and relocated  we tried multiple times to sedate the  animal  and after multiple attempts were  unsuccessful we made the very difficult  decision to euthanize it for public  safety reasons  public information officer ashley  sanchez says after being in that complex  for some time they were worried about  someone getting hurt  we're in an apartment complex where  there's people around  and anything can happen mountain lion  sightings are up  another one was spotted this past april  so far there have been 31 sightings this  year compared to 15 in 2020  she says urban growth and drought  conditions are contributing factors  whether it's pools fountains golf  courses  there's abundant food and water in  neighborhoods which they're not able to  find  in the wild right now  now back out here live the department  does expect to get more reports of  sightings as this drought continues  they are asking people to not feed or  give water to wild animals such as those  mountain lions or even coyotes  now that mountain lion that was  euthanized here at this location was  sent to a lab  for testing reporting live in the  southwest valley joe muller 8 news now
 

